      "title": "Sketching the Slope Field of a Differential Equation at Some Isolated Points"
    },
    "question": {
      ":question/id": "529b7774-40ef-565c-9dc6-7c90bebf06fa",
      ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
      ":question/problem": "Which of the following diagrams gives the slope field at the points indicated for the differential equation $\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}=1\\frac{y^{2}}{x}?$",
      ":question/worked-solution": "We have to sketch the slope field at the points $(-2,1)$,$(1,2)$,$(3,-1)$.\n\nTo sketch the slope field at the given points, we substitute their coordinates into the right-hand side of the differential equation.\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned} & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(-2,1)}=1\\frac{(1)^{2}}{(-2)}=- \\frac{1}{2}, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(1,2)}=1\\frac{(2)^{2}}{(1)}=4, \\\\  & {\\frac{\\text{d}y}{\\text{d}x}|}_{(3,-1)}=1\\frac{(-1)^{2}}{(3)}=\\frac{1}{3}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSetting up our points and drawing our line segments, we get the following diagram.\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/generate/repairs/2026-10-20-early-cohort/jobs/17592186172038/review-3-policy-2-b6fc68f59ef8c5ba/assets/63c6b174d7139dfa1325946e6ccb4c9f2d2eceef3b4c105143287c1301b2145e.png)",
